---
import Guide from '../../layouts/Guide.astro'
import Capture from '../../components/Capture.astro'
import { doc, shot } from '../../lib/content'
const sections: [string, string][] = [
  ['setup', '1. Enable Brain'],
  ['queue', '2. Read a lane'],
  ['changes', '3. See what changed'],
  ['explore', '4. Walk the map'],
  ['recall', '5. Ask a question'],
  ['privacy', 'Data and recovery'],
]
---

<Guide
  title="Build a Brain you can inspect"
  description="Save decisions from a lane and follow its relationships on the map. See why Brain Agent made a change. Recall the evidence in your next lane."
  sections={sections}
  eyebrow="Guide / Desktop knowledge"
>
  <section id="setup">
    <h2>1. Enable Brain on this computer</h2>
    <p>
      Brain is off by default and Desktop-only. It learns from lanes you choose, not every
      conversation on your machine.
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li>
        Open <strong>Settings → Brain</strong> and read the terms for the local embedding model.
      </li>
      <li>Turn on Brain and wait for the download. Search runs on this computer.</li>
      <li>
        Leave <strong>Recall in lanes</strong> on if you want agents in new local lanes to find memories.
        Restart an existing lane to give it recall.
      </li>
      <li>Choose <strong>Open Brain</strong>. You can also open it from the sidebar.</li>
    </ol>
    <Capture
      image={shot('brain-ready')}
      alt="Brain enabled in Settings with recall and automatic checks switched on."
      caption="Choose whether Brain Agent checks new knowledge on its own"
    />
  </section>
  <section id="queue">
    <h2>2. Read a lane</h2>
    <p>
      Open a local lane’s menu and choose <strong>Queue for knowledge extraction</strong>. Brain
      waits for a busy lane to settle. It reads user and assistant text, not tool output. Use the
      same menu to read just the new messages, or choose <strong>Read again for Brain</strong> to read
      the whole lane.
    </p>
    <p>
      A finished read saves at once. Personal claims never become memories. Temporary claims end on
      their stated date, or after 14 days if there is none. The status bar shows the read in
      progress and how many lanes are queued. If a read fails, retry it or dismiss it.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="changes">
    <h2>3. See what changed</h2>
    <p>
      Every saved read appears in <strong>What changed</strong>. Open its “How it was read” record
      for the claims, calls and scrubbed source. Use <strong>Undo</strong> to remove what that read added
      and restore what it replaced, unless later work depends on it.
    </p>
    <Capture
      image={shot('brain-changes')}
      alt="A saved Harbor read with Undo beside it. A Brain Agent run records why it linked Tom Lee to Rina Okafor."
      caption="The read and the link are separate changes"
    />
    <p>
      About a minute after reads settle, Brain Agent checks what waits. It links people, groups what
      belongs together and settles repeats. Each change has a reason. Undo a change or an eligible
      run. Erasure is permanent.
    </p>
    <p>
      Brain Agent starts at most six automatic checks in 24 hours. Turn them off in Settings → Brain
      if you prefer. <strong>Defrag</strong> runs the same check on request when something waits.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="explore">
    <h2>4. Walk the map</h2>
    <p>
      The map starts with you. If Brain does not know you yet, choose <strong>This is me</strong> on your
      card. Hover over a link to read its sentence: “Tom Lee took over Harbor ledger from Rina Okafor.”
      A link can say since when, until when, or both.
    </p>
    <Capture
      image={shot('brain-graph')}
      alt="A map centred on Rina Okafor, with her relationship sentences and the memories behind them on her card."
      caption="Start with a person, then follow a sentence"
    />
    <p>
      A card shows current relationships under <strong>Now</strong> and ended ones under <strong
        >Before</strong
      >. Ended links stay on the map as dashed lines. “Appear together” means a memory names both
      things, not that it explains the connection.
    </p>
    <p>
      Click a sentence to walk to the other end. Back retraces your steps. Open a memory to check
      its quotes, or choose <strong>Whole graph</strong> to see beyond this neighbourhood.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="recall">
    <h2>5. Ask a question</h2>
    <p>
      Type “Who took over billing for Harbor?” and choose <strong>Ask</strong>. The answer lists
      memories and lights their trail on the map. To send an instruction instead, choose <strong
        >Tell Brain Agent</strong
      >. Asking does not edit the graph.
    </p>
    <Capture
      image={shot('brain-recall')}
      alt="An Ask answer about the Ledger handover beside the highlighted connections on the map."
      caption="Follow the answer back to the memories that support it"
    />
    <p>
      Agents in lanes use one read-only tool, <code>brain_search</code>, to walk the same knowledge.
      They can ask a question, start with a person or thing and some context, or trace how two names
      connect. Recall is earlier context to verify, not an instruction. A lane can flag a memory for
      Brain Agent to check, but cannot change it.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="privacy">
    <h2>What stays here and what leaves</h2>
    <p>
      The graph and search index stay on this machine. Brain does not sync to Hosts or other
      Desktops and does not run in the web app. Local storage does not mean fully offline.
    </p>
    <p>
      Reading sends scrubbed lane text to the reader’s provider. Scrubbing can miss sensitive
      details. Use <strong>Never let Brain read this lane</strong> to exclude a lane. During automatic
      checks or Defrag, memories, names and scrubbed excerpts that need a decision can go to the Brain
      lane’s model. Brain Agent can check sources through that lane’s connectors, read-only. Recalled
      knowledge can enter a later lane’s provider context.
    </p>
    <p>
      Turning Brain off pauses reads and keeps its knowledge. <strong>Forget</strong> permanently removes
      a memory, not the source transcript. <strong>Reset Brain knowledge</strong> clears knowledge and
      read records after confirmation. Settings and the downloaded model stay.
    </p>
    <p>
      <a href="/watch/#brain-walkthrough">Watch the text-led walkthrough</a> · <a
        href={doc('brain.md')}>Brain’s storage and recall contract</a
      > · <a href="/privacy/#brain">Privacy</a>
    </p>
  </section>
</Guide>
